Catalog data — OpenNGC (CC BY-SA 4.0)
-------------------------------------
The deep-sky catalog (Messier + NGC/IC, ~12,000 objects) is derived from
OpenNGC:

    OpenNGC — a dataset of NGC and IC objects
    https://github.com/mattiaverga/OpenNGC
    © (see the OpenNGC repository for authors), licensed under the
    Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International License
    (CC BY-SA 4.0). To view a copy of this license, visit
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

The Messier designations themselves are public-domain (Charles Messier's
original catalog, 18th century).

Changes made to the OpenNGC-derived data: field selection and light
normalization to the application's data model; no object data was invented.

Popularity data — the popularity source
-----------------------------
The community popularity counts that rank the catalog (and select the 200
"standard mode" targets) originate from the popularity source:

    https://popularity-source.invalid/

They are shipped as a ONE-TIME snapshot baked into the application's catalog
data (`app/popularity_data.py` -> `web/catalog.json`). Neither NightSeam shell
contacts the popularity source at runtime; the snapshot is refreshed deliberately by the
maintainers with `scripts/match_popularity.py --live`.

Astronomy algorithms
--------------------
Sun/moon positions, twilight, refraction and precession are implemented
after published algorithms in:

    J. Meeus, "Astronomical Algorithms", 2nd ed., Willmann-Bell, 1998

(the algorithms themselves are mathematical facts and not copyrightable;
this implementation is original code.)

Zenith sky-brightness estimate — EOG VIIRS Nighttime Lights
------------------------------------------------------------
The optional zenith sky-brightness estimate is derived from the Earth
Observation Group's Annual VNL nighttime-lights products:

    Earth Observation Group, Annual VNL products
    https://eogdata.mines.edu/products/vnl/
    Product license and attribution terms:
    https://eogdata.mines.edu/files/EOG_products_CC_License.pdf

The shared estimator also implements the published simplified VIIRS sky-glow
relationship from Duriscoe et al., "A simplified model of all-sky artificial
sky glow derived from VIIRS Day/Night band data":

    https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jqsrt.2018.04.028

NightSeam does not download or redistribute the raw EOG rasters at runtime.
An operator-built, reduced pack is optional and keeps its EOG source year and
filenames in a sidecar metadata file. The Lorenz sky-brightness atlas is not a
runtime input or shipped asset; it is permitted only as an external validation
reference when a maintainer supplies comparison points.

Object example images — Wikimedia
---------------------------------
Example photos of objects are fetched at runtime from Wikipedia /
Wikimedia Commons, and a subset is bundled with the web version. Each image
is the work of its individual author and remains under its own license
(typically CC BY-SA, CC BY, or public domain). Per-image credit and license
details are available on the file pages:

    https://commons.wikimedia.org/
    (search for the object's name, e.g. "Messier 42")

